<!--CLI Documentation — Installation
  FQN: com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation.Installation-->
<clidoc:Installation xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:clidoc="com.machineblocks.bml.cli.documentation">

  <baseType>
    core:Documentation
  </baseType>
  <is>
    How MachineBlocks users install the host
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Consumer project (the usual path):

        npx @machineblocks/cli init com.example.bml
        npx machineblocks validate
        npx machineblocks compile com.example.bml.bricks.MyBrick

    init calls the universal project scaffold, then records @machineblocks/core
    in dependencies beside @blockml/core, records @machineblocks/cli as a
    devDependency, and adds com.machineblocks.bml.Library to the project
    library. Preflight runs inside that scaffold before any write. A BlockML
    project keeps AGENTS.md, and generation overwrites it after npm install.
    Otherwise an existing AGENTS.md is renamed to AGENTS.md.pre-blockml.
    By default npm install then runs and AGENTS.md is generated the same way
    blockml init does. The profile is recorded before npm install.
    --no-install skips npm install and AGENTS.md generation and does not skip
    preflight. On a project that blockml init already created, the same
    command adds only the missing profile. BML files and CLAUDE.md stay.

    Point an existing project at this host:

        npx @machineblocks/cli update

    update rewrites @machineblocks/cli, @machineblocks/core, @blockml/cli, and
    @blockml/core to the versions of the invoked CLI, then runs npm install.
    It writes package.json only. --no-install skips npm install. A directory
    whose package.json has no blockml key exits 2 and writes nothing.

    That install brings @machineblocks/render-target and
    @blockml/block-render-engine, plus the BlockML compiler packages.
    The render target peers @machineblocks/core, so the project dependency
    selects the installed core version. A bml segment in the package FQN lets
    compile map the output package to scad.

    From this monorepo while the bin is not published:

        npm install
        npx blockml validate

    The second command checks these BML libraries. It is the toolchain CLI used
    to develop this repo, not the MachineBlocks user command.

    Requires Node.js 20 or newer.

    Domain users should not be sent to npm link of the blockml monorepo. The
    universal installation note that says "install @machineblocks/cli from the
    MachineBlocks mono-repo" now means this package (@machineblocks/cli in the
    machineblocks-renderer repository). @machineblocks/core remains the separate
    domain library.

  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="clidoc:Introduction" relation="SeeAlso" />
  </references>
</clidoc:Installation>
